 1. Swapping first and last byte using pointers

A `char *` moves one byte at a time, so it can be used to access the individual bytes of an `int`.


#include <stdio.h>

int main()
{
    int num = 0x12345678;
    char *p = (char *)&num;   // points to 1st byte
    char *q = p + 3;          // points to last byte (4-byte int)
    char t;

    t  = *p;
    *p = *q;
    *q = t;

    printf("%x\n", num);
    return 0;
}




 2. Pre/post increment with pointers

Setup (assume `&i` is `1000`, `int` is 4 bytes):


int i = 10, j;
int *p, *q;
p = &i;      // p holds 1000


| Statement     | Type                | What happens | Result |

| `q = p++;`    | Post-inc of address | First assign, then increment. `q` gets 1000, then `p` becomes 1004  | `q = 1000`, `p = 1004` |
| `j = *p++;`   | Post-inc of address | `j` gets `*p` (10), then `p` becomes 1004                           | `j = 10`, `p = 1004`   |
| `j = *++p;`   | Pre-inc of address  | `p` becomes 1004 first, then `j` gets the value at 1004             | `j` = garbage value    |
| `j = ++*p;`   | Pre-inc of value    | `*p` is incremented first (10 → 11), then assigned                  | `j = 11`, `i = 11`     |
| `j = ++(*p);` | Pre-inc of value    | Same as above                                                       | `j = 11`, `i = 11`     |
| `j = (*p)++;` | Post-inc of value   | `j` gets 10, then `*p` is incremented                               |`j = 10`, `i = 11`      |

> Tip: Postfix `++` has higher precedence than `*`, so `*p++` means `*(p++)`. Use `(*p)++` to increment the value.



 3. `const` with pointers

| Expression | Increments | `const int *p`   | `int *const p`   | `const int *const p`    |
                              (value fixed)    (address fixed)     (address & value fixed)
                              
| `*p++`     | address    | Valid            | Error            | Error                   |
| `*++p`     | address    | Valid            | Error            | Error                   |
| `(*p)++`   | value      | Error            | Valid            | Error                   |
| `++(*p)`   | value      | Error            | Valid            | Error                   |
| `++*p`     | value      | Error            | Valid            | Error                   |

- `const int *p`: the value can't be changed through `p`, but `p` can point elsewhere.
- `int *const p`: `p` can't point elsewhere, but the value can be changed.
- `const int *const p`: neither the address nor the value can be changed.



 4. Void pointer (generic pointer)

A `void *` has no specific data type, but it can be used like a pointer of any type. That is why it is called a 'generic pointer'.

> Note: At the time of dereferencing, a void pointer must be explicitly type converted (cast), otherwise it gives a compile error.


#include <stdio.h>

int main()
{
    int i = 10;
    char ch = 'a';
    float f = 23.5;
    void *p;

    p = &i;
    printf("%d\n", *(int *)p);     // 10

    p = &ch;
    printf("%c\n", *(char *)p);    // a

    p = &f;
    printf("%f\n", *(float *)p);   // 23.500000

    return 0;
}


In `*(float *)p`, the `(float *)` part is the 'type conversion' and the leading `*` is the 'dereference'.



 5. NULL pointer

When a pointer is holding `0` as its address, it is called a 'NULL pointer'.

Application: a NULL pointer is used to avoid **wild pointers** and 'dangling pointers'.


int main()
{
    int *p;
    p = 0;       // p = NULL (null pointer)
    return 0;
}


As per the C programming standard, whenever our work with a pointer is completed, make it `NULL`.



 6. Arithmetic operations on pointers


int *p = (int *)1000;
int *q = (int *)1020;

printf("%ld\n", q - p);   // 5


The memory difference between the two addresses is `1020 - 1000 = 20` bytes. Since `int` is 4 bytes, `20 / 4 = 5`.

(Side note from the notes: if `q` were `1015`, the result would be `15 / 4 = 3`, truncated.)

Adding, multiplying or dividing two pointers is not allowed:


printf("%ld\n", q + p);   // error
printf("%ld\n", q * p);   // error
printf("%ld\n", q / p);   // error


> Only subtraction of two pointers is valid, and both pointers must be of the same type.
